combination of `DISTINCT` + `ORDER BY DESC` + `LIMIT` (with `LIMIT` below a certain threshold).\\n\\n### To Reproduce\\n\\n```sql\\n-- DuckDB v1.5.1 & v1.5.2 on Windows, 17.6M rows table with 8643 distinct DATE values\\n\\n-- Bug: returns only 1 row\\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 3000;\\n-- Result: 1 row (expected: 3000)\\n\\n-- All these work correctly:\\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date ASC  LIMIT 3000;  -- OK: 3000 rows\\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC;            -- OK: 8643 rows (no LIMIT)\\nSELECT DISTINCT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 5000; -- OK: 5000 rows (large LIMIT)\\nSELECT date FROM market.qmt_daily_qfq GROUP BY date ORDER BY date DESC LIMIT 3000; -- OK: 3000 rows (GROUP BY workaround)\\n```\\n\\nThe threshold where the bug stops occurring is between ~3400\\u20133500 in my test case (8643 distinct dates, 17.6M rows). The bug is **deterministic** \\u2014 same result every run with fresh connections.\\n\\n### OS:\\n\\nWindows 11\\n\\n### DuckDB Version:\\n\\nv1.5.1 and v1.5.2 (Python package)\\n\\n### DuckDB Client:\\n\\nPython\\n\\n### Hardware:\\n\\n14 CPU threads\\n\\n### Full Name:\\n\\nxuyang7952\\n\\n### Affiliation:\\n\\nPersonal\\n\\n### Did you include all relevant configuration (e.g., CPU architecture, Linux distribution) to reproduce the issue?\\n\\n- [ ] Yes, I have\\n\\n### Did you include all code required to reproduce the issue?\\n\\n- [ ] Yes, I have\\n\\n### Did you include all relevant data sets for reproducing the issue?\\n\\nNo - Other reason (please specify in the issue body)\"}\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
